    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d486">

                <pb facs="0583" n="573" sameAs="frus:frus1947v07/pg573"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/10–647: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Tihwa</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Paxton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tihwa</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-10-06T09:00:00+06:00">October 6, 1947—9
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received October 7—4:51 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>57. Yesterday informed by Garrison Commander Sung that delayed news recently
                    received by courier from Osman, in absence of radio communications, that
                    Chenghua (remytel to Nanking 367, repeated Washington 49, September 23) fell to
                    his forces about September 21. Opposing leaders have fled leaving all important
                    places in Ashan zone under Osman’s control.</p>
                <p>Sung maintains that no Chinese regular troops have been engaged in this fighting,
                    [garbled group] cavalry (see page 7 of memo enclosed Tihwa despatch to
                    Department 20, June 30<anchor n="42" xml:id="d486-f1"/>) remaining at
                    Peitashan.</p>
                <p>Sent Nanking 379, repeated Department.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paxton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
